      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "5375ce10-c55e-50f6-bf25-d6e65f44a47f",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Projects: Sandy and Beaver Canal, Los Angeles Aqueduct system\n- Organizations: Los Angeles Department of Water and Power, American Society of Civil Engineers\n- Places: Bolivar, Ohio, Glasgow, Pennsylvania, Lisbon, Ohio, Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains, Los Angeles, California\n\nFacts:\n- The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 miles from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\n- The Sandy and Beaver Canal ceased operating in 1852 after the Cold Run Reservoir Dam outside Lisbon, Ohio, broke and ruined a large portion of the canal.\n- The Los Angeles Aqueduct system comprises the Los Angeles Aqueduct, also called the Owens Valley aqueduct, and the Second Los Angeles Aqueduct.\n- The Los Angeles Department of Water and Power built and operates the Los Angeles Aqueduct system.\n- The Los Angeles Aqueduct system delivers water from the Owens River in the Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains to Los Angeles, California.\n- In 1971, the American Society of Civil Engineers recognized the Los Angeles Aqueduct system on its List of Historic Civil Engineering Landmarks.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "d09270ed-7039-5188-b613-995484cb7f4e",
        "text": "Page: Sandy and Beaver Canal. Sentence: The Sandy and Beaver Canal ran 73 mi from the Ohio and Erie Canal at Bolivar, Ohio, to the Ohio River at Glasgow, Pennsylvania.\nPage: Sandy and Beaver Canal. Sentence: The canal ceased to operate in 1852, when the Cold Run Reservoir Dam outside of Lisbon, Ohio, broke, ruining a large portion of the canal.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: The Los Angeles Aqueduct system, comprising the Los Angeles Aqueduct (Owens Valley aqueduct) and the Second Los Angeles Aqueduct, is a water conveyance system, built and operated by the Los Angeles Department of Water and Power.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: The system delivers water from the Owens River in the Eastern Sierra Nevada Mountains to Los Angeles, California.\nPage: Los Angeles Aqueduct. Sentence: In 1971 it was recognized by the American Society of Civil Engineers on the List of Historic Civil Engineering Landmarks.",
